{"id":"q13","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"puzzle","question":"Two trains start 100 miles apart and head toward each other at 50 mph each. A fly flies back and forth between them at 75 mph until they meet. Does the fly travel 75 miles?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The trains close the 100-mile gap at a combined 100 mph, so they meet after 1 hour.","confidence":0.99,"lean":1,"theta":1.5551,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The fly flies at a constant 75 mph for one hour, so it covers 75 miles.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The time-based shortcut works regardless of how many times the fly turns around.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Summing the fly's zigzag legs gives an infinite series of ever-shorter trips that is hard to sum.","confidence":0.6,"lean":-0.2,"theta":0.9425,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The difficulty of summing the series is a difficulty of method and not a reason the answer would differ.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The infinite series converges to 75 miles, matching the shortcut.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Real flies need time to turn and vary in speed, which could change the distance.","confidence":0.2,"lean":-0.5,"theta":0.3142,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question assumes idealized instant turns and constant speed, so real-world deviations do not matter.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The fly's travel time equals the trains' meeting time, and distance equals speed times time, giving 75 miles.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":-0.4,"re